### How does iterate generate powers of two?

`Stream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2)` starts with the seed value 1 and applies the doubling function to produce each subsequent value: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on. Each power of two depends only on the single previous value, so the unary operator on one integer suffices. No pair encoding is needed, unlike Fibonacci.

### How do you implement it?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class PowersOfTwo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Long> powers = Stream.iterate(1L, n -> n * 2)
            .limit(10)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Powers of 2: " + powers);

        // Banking use case: exponential backoff delays in milliseconds
        List<Long> backoffDelays = Stream.iterate(100L, d -> d * 2)
            .limit(8)
            .toList();
        System.out.println("Backoff delays (ms): " + backoffDelays);
    }
}
```

```text
Powers of 2: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512]
Backoff delays (ms): [100, 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200, 6400, 12800]
```

### How is this different from Fibonacci?

| Aspect | Powers of 2 | Fibonacci |
|---|---|---|
| Predecessors needed | 1 (just double the last) | 2 (sum of last two) |
| State carried | Single value | Pair {a, b} |
| Operator | `n -> n * 2` | `{a,b} -> {b, a+b}` |
| Map step needed | No | Yes: `map(f -> f[0])` |

### What about overflow?

```java
// int overflows at the 31st power of 2
int intMax = Integer.MAX_VALUE;  // 2^31 - 1 = 2,147,483,647

// long overflows at the 63rd power of 2
long longMax = Long.MAX_VALUE;   // 2^63 - 1

// BigInteger for arbitrary precision
import java.math.BigInteger;
List<BigInteger> largePowers = Stream.iterate(
        BigInteger.ONE, n -> n.multiply(BigInteger.TWO))
    .limit(100)
    .toList();
// No overflow even at 2^99
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Forgetting to bound the infinite stream.

```java
// WRONG: no limit, runs forever
Stream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2)
    .forEach(System.out::println);  // never terminates

// CORRECT: always limit infinite streams
Stream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2)
    .limit(10)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

### What about the three-argument iterate (Java 9)?

```java
// Powers of 2 up to 1000
List<Integer> powersUpTo1000 = Stream.iterate(1, n -> n <= 1000, n -> n * 2)
    .toList();
// [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512]
// Self-terminating: no limit needed
```

### What about using IntStream for primitives?

```java
// Avoid boxing with IntStream
int[] powers = java.util.stream.IntStream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2)
    .limit(10)
    .toArray();
// Returns int[] directly, no Integer boxing
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The contrast with Fibonacci illustrates a key principle: the amount of state carried in `iterate` is dictated by how many predecessors the recurrence needs. Doubling needs only one predecessor, so a single value suffices. Fibonacci needs two, so a pair is required. This generalizes to any k-order recurrence.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why is no pair needed here?" Doubling depends on only the last value, not the last two.
- "When does long overflow?" At 2^63.
- "How do you generate powers of 3?" Change the operator to `n -> n * 3`.
- "How do you avoid boxing?" Use `IntStream.iterate` or `LongStream.iterate`.
